       <li><strong>Ray trace.</strong> Paraxial rays (y, n·θ) pushed through the same round-trip matrices, so they exist whether or not a Gaussian eigenmode does. The round-trip Gouy phase θ = arccos m sets how fast a ray circles the axis; if θ/2π is close to a fraction p/q with small q the ray closes on itself after q round trips and transverse modes become degenerate. For |m| &gt; 1 a ray grows by |m| + √(m² − 1) per round trip. Apertures only stop rays; their diffraction loss is not modelled.</li>
       <li><strong>Astigmatism compensation.</strong> The button scans one common incidence angle θ for all curved mirrors and keeps the one where both planes see the same cavity, m<sub>T</sub> = m<sub>S</sub> (equal stability ranges, round beam in the arms). For comparison it quotes the textbook two-mirror condition for the Brewster crystal, R sin θ tan θ = L(n²−1)/n³ with L the path length in the crystal (Kogelnik et al. 1972). Brewster surfaces elsewhere (BRF, plates) also enter the scan.</li>
       <li><strong>Layout.</strong> A four-mirror ring is drawn as a bowtie from the real arm lengths, with the crystal arm at the bottom and the two arms horizontal; only the horizontal direction is stretched to fill the panel. The fold angles that close this bowtie are compared with the incidence angles you entered, which are what the beam calculation uses.</li>
+      <li><strong>Spatial hole burning panel.</strong> One mode q lases alone; its gain is clamped so that its own modal gain equals the loss. Along the crystal the saturated gain is g(z) = g₀/(1 + S·I(z)), with the standing-wave intensity I = sin²(k p) in a linear cavity and I = 1 in a ring with an isolator; S follows from the clamping condition. Mode q + m sees the overlap of g(z) with its own pattern, which is phase-shifted by 2π m p/p<sub>rt</sub>, times its own small-signal gain and loss (gain line and filters). A bar above 1 means that mode can start. Pump absorption along the crystal, transverse profiles and the second mode's own saturation are ignored. In a ring without an isolator the standing-wave position is not pinned by a mirror; the panel assumes equal counter-propagating beams whose patterns coincide at the crystal centre. A twisted-mode pair is treated as making the intensity in the crystal uniform. The mode count in the results uses the simpler rule under Mode selection, so the two can differ.</li>
       <li><strong>Thermal lens.</strong> Thin lens at the crystal centre, f = πKw<sub>p</sub>²/(P<sub>heat</sub>·dn/dT) with P<sub>heat</sub> = quantum defect × absorbed pump. It ignores end-face bulging, stress birefringence and aberrations, and overestimates the lens for very tight pump spots, so use the calibration factor if you have a measured value. The Ti:sapphire example uses 0.3 as an assumption, not a measurement.</li>
       <li><strong>Threshold and power.</strong> Ideal four-level rate-equation estimate: P<sub>th</sub> = hν<sub>p</sub>π(w<sub>l</sub>²+w<sub>p</sub>²)γ / (2·N<sub>pass</sub>·σ·τ), slope = (λ<sub>p</sub>/λ<sub>l</sub>)·η<sub>overlap</sub>·0.9·T/γ. No excited-state absorption, upconversion or reabsorption, so thresholds are optimistic.</li>
       <li><strong>Mode selection.</strong> Every cavity mode inside the gain window is given a ratio x = (gain)/(round-trip loss incl. filter losses). The best mode wins. In a travelling-wave ring (ring + optical isolator) homogeneous broadening allows only that one mode; the margin to the runner-up tells you how prone it is to mode hops. With spatial hole burning (linear cavity, or ring without an isolator) modes whose x is within half of the excess over threshold also lase; a twisted-mode pair reduces this to 10 %.</li>
